# Day 27 — port_scanner

A simple **TCP port scanner** for `localhost` (`127.0.0.1`).

## ⚠️ Legal and Ethical Warning

**Only scan systems you own or have explicit written permission to scan.**

Scanning networks or systems you don't own is illegal in many
jurisdictions and violates most terms of service. This program
is for **educational purposes only**, and only on your own machine.

## What Is Port Scanning?

A port scanner tries to connect to each port on a host to see
which ones have services listening.

| Result | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| **OPEN** | A service is listening on this port |
| **CLOSED** | No service (or firewall blocking) |

Port scanning is the first step in **reconnaissance**—the process
of gathering information about a target before an attack.

## Usage

    ./port_scanner <start_port> <end_port>

- Ports must be in the range 1–65535.
- `start_port` must be less than or equal to `end_port`.

## Example

    $ ./port_scanner 1 1024
    ====================================
             PORT SCANNER
    ====================================

    Target: 127.0.0.1
    Range:  1–1024

    ⚠️  Only scan systems you own.

    Port    22: OPEN  (SSH)
    Port    80: OPEN  (HTTP)
    Port   443: OPEN  (HTTPS)

    Scan complete. 3 open port(s) found.

